ical for November 13.","t_rec":"Charlotte is setting record highs far faster than chance","p_rec":"If Charlotte's climate weren't changing, every year would have the same chance of holding a daily record. Since 2000, Charlotte has set <strong>78 standing record lows</strong>, close to the 66 that chance alone would give, and <strong class=\"sig\">101 record highs</strong>, far more than the 66 that chance alone would give. The 1950s hold the most record highs of any decade (46). Charlotte's all-time high is 104\u00b0F, on September 6, 1954.","t_chg":"Charlotte's days got warmer. Its nights didn't.","p_chg":"Across 147 complete years: Charlotte's average low shows no clear change: \u22120.04\u00b0F per decade, a range (\u22120.09 to +0.02) that includes zero. Charlotte's average high has risen <strong class='sig'>+0.22\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.17 to +0.27), about +3.2\u00b0F since 1879.","t_mon":"Lows rose in 2 of 12 months; January fell. Highs rose in 10 of 12 months.","t_rain":"Charlotte has gotten drier","p_rain":"Charlotte's yearly precipitation has fallen <strong class='sig'>\u22120.36 inches per decade</strong> (95% range \u22120.67 to \u22120.06), about \u22125.3 inches since 1879. The 1991\u20132020 normal is 43.6 inches a year. The wettest year was 1884 (68.44 in); the driest, 2001 (26.23 in).","show_snow":true,"sig_bars":{"lo":false,"hi":true},"t_year":"2026 so far: 12 daily record highs, 1 daily record low"}}